Massively, because once synthetic worlds become persistent, adaptive, and socially shared, they stop being “content” and start becoming institutions.

Culture changes first. People begin forming identities around synthetic leagues, characters, factions, histories, styles, rituals, and accomplishments the same way they already do around sports teams, fandoms, schools, brands, professions, and nations. The difference is that these worlds can evolve continuously and respond to their communities. Culture becomes more participatory: people do not just consume myths; they help instantiate, remix, and maintain them.

Markets follow attention. Wherever people care, scarcity and exchange appear. You get media rights, sponsorships, virtual goods, analytics, coaching, scouting, creator tools, licensing, merchandising, talent markets, prediction markets where lawful, collectibles, experiences, and entirely new service categories. A synthetic athlete or fictional franchise could accumulate economic value because millions of humans care about its history.

Status changes too. Today status often tracks things like wealth, athletic ability, celebrity, credentials, ownership, taste, and social reach. In persistent synthetic worlds you add new status surfaces: who discovered the best strategy, who designed the most influential world, whose agent became legendary, who owns or stewards a famous franchise, who created a rule set everyone copied, who has the deepest historical knowledge, who is trusted as a curator, who has the most respected lineage of contributions.

That creates a feedback loop:

\text{attention}
\rightarrow
\text{meaning}
\rightarrow
\text{status}
\rightarrow
\text{economic value}
\rightarrow
\text{more creation}
\rightarrow
\text{more attention}

And because these worlds can preserve provenance and history, status can become path-dependent. Being early matters. Origin stories matter. Lineage matters. “I created that mechanic” or “my grandfather’s story became the basis for this faction” can become culturally valuable in the same way provenance matters in art, sports, science, and institutions now.

The interesting part is that the boundary between “real” and “fictional” economic value gets weaker. If a fictional league employs real artists, programmers, commentators, sponsors, analysts, traders, and viewers, then its surrounding economy is completely real even if the underlying athletes are synthetic.

So the deeper rule is:

\boxed{\text{shared belief + persistent history + scarce attention} \rightarrow \text{real social and economic consequences}}

That has always been true of culture. These systems just make the process much more programmable, recursive, and fast-moving.

And if the worlds keep learning from deltas the way we were describing, then culture itself starts becoming iteratively engineered rather than merely inherited. That is probably where the largest effects land.